# The catalogue's own film notes, verbatim. Read 15 September 2026 from
# /service/search/catalog/item/<catalog>, one call per parish, and checked
# against a second fetch by hash before being written down.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ANSWER TO «A FILM NUMBER IS NOT A BOOK», and the answer is
# not that the catalogue is vague. It is that the catalogue's unit is the ITEM
# and the viewer's unit is the ROLL.
#
# A physical roll of microfilm carries several items, and the items on one roll
# need not come from one parish. Karlobag's baptisms 1691-1804 are DGS 5481648,
# and the catalogue says exactly what they are: ITEMS 4 - 5 of roll 1419107.
# Items 1 - 3 of that same roll are KAPTOL (koha:655210, 300km inland). So
# /search/film/005481648 opens the roll at item 1 and lands in Kaptol, which is
# what happened here and cost a sitting. The Karlobag baptisms are not reachable
# by that number at all: they are behind the waypoint
# 9RK1-3Y9:391644801,391849401,391849402, 327 images.
#
# The same shape explains Otocac. DGS 5482656 is ITEM 6 of roll 1536689 — five
# items of something else come first — and DGS 5482657 is catalogued as births
# AND deaths because it is items 1-2, two separate books, which is why walking
# it gives 483 images of baptisms and then END OF ROLL.
#
# And a book can have TWO film numbers. Ledenice's registers were filmed twice:
# roll 1419155 (DGS 5482218) and roll 2119536 (DGS 5498069) are the same three
# books, decades apart. Neither is wrong; they are two photographs of one shelf.
#
# THIRTY-ONE OF THESE EIGHTY-TWO FILMS DO NOT BEGIN AT ITEM 1. Those are the
# ones a film number cannot reach. Trsat's oldest register is item 8; Bribir's
# is item 7; Karlobag's baptisms are items 4-5. Seventeen of the nineteen
# parishes have at least one. tools/holdingscheck.py counts them on every run.
#
# So: cite a parish and a DATE RANGE from this file, and cite an ARK for the
# image. Never treat a DGS number as an address.
#
# parish|catalog|dgs|roll|items|contents
